<h2>How tornado coverage typically works</h2>

<h3>Windstorm is the covered peril</h3>

<p>Most homeowners and commercial property policies do not list tornado separately. A tornado is windstorm, and windstorm is generally a covered peril. Dallas County is not in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) area, so the coverage normally comes from your regular insurer, or from the Texas FAIR Plan where that applies. Rain that enters through a tornado-made opening is usually treated as part of the wind loss, while flooding from overflowing creeks is a separate peril typically excluded by standard policies.</p>

<h3>The deductible</h3>

<p>The wind/hail deductible usually applies. On many Texas policies it is a percentage of the Coverage A dwelling limit, such as 1% or 2%, so it can be several thousand dollars. On a total loss the deductible is a smaller share of the claim, but on a partial loss it can decide whether minor damage is worth claiming. Your declarations page shows the exact figure.</p>

<h3>The coverages that come into play</h3>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>What it generally addresses after a tornado</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dwelling (Coverage A)</td><td>Repair or rebuilding of the home's structure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other structures (Coverage B)</td><td>Detached garages, fences, sheds, carports</td></tr>
<tr><td>Personal property (Coverage C)</td><td>Furniture, clothing, electronics, and other contents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loss of use (Coverage D)</td><td>Reasonable extra living costs while the home is unlivable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debris removal</td><td>Hauling away debris from covered damaged property, often with limits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinance or law</td><td>Increased costs to meet current building codes, up to its limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business income (commercial)</td><td>Lost income during the restoration period, if purchased</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Coverage names, limits, and conditions vary by policy form. Read your own policy, or have someone read it with you, before you accept a settlement.</p>

<h2>Steps after a tornado in Dallas County</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Put safety first.</strong> Stay out of damaged structures until they are safe. Watch for downed lines and report them to Oncor, and report gas odors to Atmos Energy.</li>
<li><strong>Document before cleanup.</strong> Take wide shots of the property and street, then close-ups of every room, roof slope, and damaged item. Video walkthroughs help.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the property.</strong> Tarp roofs, board windows, and secure the site. Keep receipts; reasonable emergency repairs are usually reimbursable.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and ask about an advance for living expenses if you cannot stay in the home.</li>
<li><strong>Start a contents inventory.</strong> List items room by room, with approximate age and replacement cost. Photos, receipts, and bank or card statements help. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> is a good starting point.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim diary</strong> of calls, emails, adjuster visits, and promises made.</li>
<li><strong>Be careful with contractors</strong> who arrive uninvited. Get written estimates and check references before signing.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Rebuilding: codes, permits, and estimates</h2>

<p>Heavy tornado damage often means rebuilding to current code. Inside the city, that runs through the City of Dallas Development Services Department under the city's adopted International Residential Code and International Building Code. Garland, Rowlett, Irving, Richardson, and other suburbs have their own permit offices. Code-required upgrades are not automatically paid by the base dwelling coverage; that depends on your ordinance or law coverage and its limit. Our guide to <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-dallas/">building codes and insurance repairs in Dallas</a> explains how this works.</p>

<h3>Partial losses on a damaged street</h3>

<p>On many tornado claims, the home is not destroyed but is damaged on several sides at once: a roof with missing sections, broken windows, dented siding or gutters, a collapsed garage door, and water stains inside from the rain that followed. These partial losses are where scope disagreements are most common. Hidden damage to framing, decking, and insulation may not show up until repairs begin, so keep the claim open and document anything new that contractors find. A supplemental request with photos and an updated estimate is a normal part of the process.</p>

<p>Large losses also bring replacement cost questions. Many policies pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are complete, often within a deadline. Mortgage companies are commonly named on claim checks and may hold funds and release them in stages as work progresses.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help with a tornado claim</h2>

<p>Tornado claims involve many moving parts, and the first estimate is often written quickly while the insurer is handling hundreds of other losses. Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], represents policyholders on <a href="/services/tornado-damage-claims/">tornado damage claims</a> and related <a href="/wind-damage-insurance-claims-dallas-tx/">wind damage claims</a> across Dallas County. That can include documenting the dwelling and contents, preparing a detailed estimate, tracking living expenses, reviewing code-related costs, and presenting the claim to the insurer.</p>

<p>A public adjuster does not do the repairs and is not a lawyer. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement, and you can rescind the contract within 72 hours of signing. Every outcome depends on the policy and the facts. If you have questions about a lawsuit, bad faith, or a deadline to sue, talk with a Texas attorney. For anything else about your claim, a Dallas Public Adjuster review is free.</p>
